# Codable: avoid custom encoders and decoders

Do **not** add hand-written `Codable` conformance via `init(from:)`, `encode(to:)`, or manual `CodingKeys` **unless there is no reasonable alternative**.

## Prefer instead

- **Synthesized `Codable`** on structs/enums when all stored properties are encodable.
- **Model shape changes** that stay synthesizable: e.g. optional properties with `nil` meaning “missing”, combined with sensible defaults at the call site or a thin migration when loading persisted data.
- **Separate types**: a persisted DTO or snapshot struct that matches storage exactly (synthesized), mapped to/from domain types in one place (e.g. store or migration), instead of encoding logic spread through domain models.
- **Encoding elsewhere**: if only one layer needs a custom wire format, implement that in the service that encodes/decodes, not on the core model.

## When custom `Codable` is acceptable

Reserve explicit `init(from:)` / `encode(to:)` for cases that truly cannot be expressed with synthesis and a mapper—e.g. an external schema you do not control.

- Keep the implementation **minimal and localized** (ideally one type dedicated to that format).
- Add a **short comment** stating why synthesis is not used.

## Examples

```swift
// ❌ Avoid: manual CodingKeys + decode for one new field when a migration or DTO would do
struct Player: Codable {
    var name: String
    var score: Int
    enum CodingKeys: String, CodingKey { case name, score, badges }
    init(from decoder: Decoder) throws { … }
}

// ✅ Prefer: optional + default when reading old data (synthesized Codable)
struct Player: Codable {
    var name: String
    var score: Int
    var badges: [String]?  // old saves omit this; treat nil as [] where used
}

// ✅ Prefer: dedicated persisted shape + mapping
struct PlayerRecord: Codable { var name: String; var score: Int; var badges: [String] }
struct Player { var name: String; var score: Int; var badges: [String] }
```

When editing persistence or models, **default to synthesis** and push compatibility concerns toward migrations, optional fields, or explicit record types—not custom `Codable` on every model.
